{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-335145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A marine rehabilitation aquarium initially houses $25$ marine animals. The number of animals in the aquarium $M(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the aquarium is $60$ animals, which differential equation could be used to model the animal population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-335145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the number of animals in the aquarium obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of animals changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of animals in the aquarium. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-M),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the aquarium, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=60$ animals, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=k(60-M).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-335145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79fa6bd7430fbd68a289c0e85accd5b6a030fd947a6dd80967fd95d1ad6e0146"} {"question" "q-335145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-335145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(150-M)"] ["math" "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(250-M)"] ["math" "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(35-M)"] ["math" "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(50-M)"] ["math" "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(60-M)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-335145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(60-M)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-335542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A wildlife reserve can support a total of $800$ deer.\nAt the beginning of a disease study in the reserve, a parasitic infection has affected $80$ deer. After $10$ days, the number of deer affected by the parasite increases to $300.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the infected deer population to reach $600$ animals?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of deer affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-335542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate at which the deer population affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total deer population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total deer population in the reserve, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ deer, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial deer population affected by the parasite was $80$ animals. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=80,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =80 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{0} & =80 \\\\ 800-K & =80 \\\\ K & =720\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-720{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the deer population affected by the parasite was $300$ animals. So, applying the condition $P(10)=300,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =300 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-10k} & =300 \\\\ 500 & =720{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{500}{720}=\\frac{25}{36} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $600$ deer are affected by the parasite. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =600 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =600 \\\\ 200 & =720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{200}{720}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $600$ deer will be affected by the parasite after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})}=35.128…≈35\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-335542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79fa6bd7430fbd68a289c0e85accd5b6a030fd947a6dd80967fd95d1ad6e0146"} {"question" "q-335542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-335542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$20$ days"] ["text" "$28$ days"] ["text" "$35$ days"] ["text" "$40$ days"] ["text" "$50$ days"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-335542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$35$ days"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-335578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Throughout an experiment in a laboratory, a culture contains $1\\,200$ bacteria in total (limited by nutrients and space). At the beginning of the experiment, $200$ of the $1\\,200$ bacteria are resistant to a new antibiotic. After $4$ hours, the number of resistant bacteria increases to $500.$ To the nearest hour, how long does it take for the number of resistant bacteria to reach $900?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of resistant bacteria, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in hours.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-335578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate at which the resistant bacteria population, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total bacteria population in the culture and its current resistant population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total bacteria population the culture can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1\\,200$ bacteria, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1\\,200-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1\\,200-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{1\\,200-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1\\,200-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $1\\,200-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,200-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 1\\,200-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 1\\,200-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =1\\,200-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial population of resistant bacteria was $200$ cells. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=200,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =200 \\\\ 1\\,200-K{e}^{0} & =200 \\\\ 1\\,200-K & =200 \\\\ K & =1\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $4$ hours, the resistant bacteria population was $500$ cells. So, applying the condition $P(4)=500,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =500 \\\\ 1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-4k} & =500 \\\\ 700 & =1\\,000{e}^{-4k} \\\\ {e}^{-4k} & =\\frac{700}{1\\,000}=\\frac{7}{10} \\\\ -4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $900$ bacteria are resistant to the antibiotic. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =900 \\\\ 1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =900 \\\\ 300 & =1\\,000{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{300}{1\\,000}=\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $900$ bacteria will be resistant to the antibiotic after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10})}{-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10})}=13.502…≈14\\,\\text{hours},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest hour." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-335578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79fa6bd7430fbd68a289c0e85accd5b6a030fd947a6dd80967fd95d1ad6e0146"} {"question" "q-335578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-335578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$10$ hours"] ["text" "$14$ hours"] ["text" "$18$ hours"] ["text" "$20$ hours"] ["text" "$8$ hours"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-335578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$14$ hours"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-344214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An ecologist found that the growth of the number of frogs in a wetland is directly proportional to the difference between the wetland’s maximum frog population and the current population. Let $F(t)$ be the frog population, measured in number of frogs, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum frog population the wetland can support is $100$ frogs, which differential equation could be used to model the frog population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-344214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate at which the frog population $F(t)$ of the wetland changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum frog population the wetland can support and the current frog population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-F),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum frog population the wetland can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=100\\text{ frogs},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(100-F).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-344214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79fa6bd7430fbd68a289c0e85accd5b6a030fd947a6dd80967fd95d1ad6e0146"} {"question" "q-344214" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-344214" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=-k(100+F)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=-k(100-F)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(100-F)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=kF(100-F)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k{F}^{2}(100-F)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-344214" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(100-F)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-344218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A school administrator observed that the growth of the number of students enrolled in a program is directly proportional to the difference between the program’s maximum enrollment capacity and the current number of students enrolled. Let $S(t)$ be the number of students enrolled, measured in students, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of students the program can support is $500$ students, which differential equation could be used to model the number of students enrolled in the program? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-344218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate at which the number of students enrolled $S(t)$ in the program changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of students the program can support and the current number of students enrolled. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-S),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of students the program can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=500\\text{ students},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(500-S).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-344218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79fa6bd7430fbd68a289c0e85accd5b6a030fd947a6dd80967fd95d1ad6e0146"} {"question" "q-344218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-344218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-k(500+S)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-k(500-S)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(500-S)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS(500-S)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k{S}^{2}(500-S)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"} {"question" "q-344218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(500-S)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062657/state.json" "file_sha256" "8480d86f601c7ddc36ad09b964ff5a13763c64b54fbd389496159d2c92a4f22e"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2004 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
